# LoRAtorio: An intrinsic approach to LoRA    Skill Composition


Niki Foteinopoulou1  Ignas Budvytis2  Stephan Liwicki1

1Cambridge Research Laboratory, Toshiba Europe

2Independent

**********Extracted claims**********

Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is a widely adopted technique in text-to-image diffusion models.
LoRA enables the personalization of visual concepts, including characters, styles, and objects.
Existing approaches struggle to effectively compose multiple LoRA adapters in open-ended settings.
LoRA adapters trained on narrow domains produce denoised outputs that diverge from the base model.
When operating out-of-distribution, LoRA outputs show behavior closer to the base model than when conditioned in distribution.
Performance is exceptional in single LoRA scenarios.
Performance deteriorates when multiple LoRA adapters are loaded simultaneously into a single model instance.
LoRAtorio achieves state-of-the-art performance.
LoRAtorio shows up to a 1.3% improvement in CLIPScore.
LoRAtorio achieves a 72.43% win rate in GPT-4V pairwise evaluations.
LoRAtorio generalizes effectively to multiple latent diffusion models.
Diffusion models operate by gradually learning to reverse a noise process.
Diffusion models effectively capture the underlying data distribution through iterative denoising.
Diffusion models approximate the complex structure of their training data.
Diffusion models generate new, previously unseen samples that remain faithful to the original data’s domain.
Works such as Dreambooth and StyleDrop have enabled personalization and fine-grained customization beyond base text-to-image generation.
Dreambooth and StyleDrop approaches often rely on LoRA adapters.
LoRA adapters specialize a base model to preserve the identity of specific concepts or objects.
LoRA adapters support applications such as virtual try-on and avatar generation.
Each LoRA adapter effectively encodes a "skill" or concept.
Generation with a single LoRA adapter yields high-quality, precise outputs.
The distribution of a LoRA-adapted model diverges from that of the base model.
Divergence is particularly evident when LoRAs are trained on narrow or highly specialized datasets.
Conditions common in personalization settings involve narrow or highly specialized datasets.
The unconditioned noise estimate produced by a LoRA differs from that of the base model.
LoRA adapters mitigate catastrophic forgetting, particularly when compared to fully fine-tuned models.
LoRAs tend to preserve the base model’s generalization capabilities.
Stylistic changes occur in generated images as a result of loaded LoRAs.
The composition and theme of the generated output more closely resemble the base model when a text condition outside the LoRA distribution is given.
The sparse and low-norm nature of LoRA weights is attributed to certain model behaviors.
When the input condition lies outside a LoRA’s target domain, the output of the augmented model more closely resembles that of the base model.
Previous works in skill composition for image generation have used both trainable and train-free approaches.
Trainable mixture-of-experts is a focus of some trainable methods.
Training a hyper-network that generates LoRA weights of a combined task is a focus of some trainable methods.
Trainable methods are impractical in real-life applications.
Trainable methods would require re-training for every new concept or domain added.
Training data may not be available in several commercial applications due to confidentiality constraints.
Inference time composition in image generation is relatively unexplored.
Inference time composition methods focus on a schedule of LoRAs based on prior knowledge.
Inference time composition methods focus on additional conditions.
Inference time composition methods focus on merging of latent space.
Weight manipulations can show diminished performance as the number of skills incorporated increases.
Unweighted merging of scores will eventually face similar issues to weight merging.
Setting a schedule for skill composition requires prior knowledge of the task.
LoRAtorio is a train-free method for multi-LoRA skill composition in image generation.
LoRAtorio leverages the intrinsic behavior of LoRA-augmented models.
LoRAtorio operates without requiring additional supervision or fine-tuning.
LoRAtorio operates in the latent space by dividing it into spatial patches.
LoRAtorio computes the cosine similarity between the output of the LoRA-augmented model and that of the base model for each patch.
Similarities are used to construct a spatially-aware weight matrix in LoRAtorio.
Patches that deviate more from the base model receive higher weights in LoRAtorio.
The spatially-aware weight matrix is used to compute a weighted average of the predicted noise outputs across LoRAs in LoRAtorio.
LoRAtorio allows the model to emphasize regions where individual LoRAs are more confident.
LoRAtorio proposes a modification to the classifier-free guidance mechanism to mitigate domain drift.
The modification incorporates the base model’s unconditional noise estimate into the weighted average.
The adjustment ensures that the final output remains grounded in the base model’s general knowledge.
LoRAtorio is entirely based on intrinsic model behavior, specifically the consistency between LoRA and base model representations.
LoRAtorio extends the task to a dynamic module selection setting.
In the dynamic module selection setting, all available LoRA adapters are loaded into the base model.
The most relevant LoRA adapters are selected ad hoc during inference in the dynamic module selection setting.
The dynamic module selection formulation more realistically reflects real-world skill composition scenarios.
In real-world skill composition scenarios, the set of required capabilities is not known a priori.
LoRAtorio is a train-free and intrinsically guided approach for multi-LoRA composition in diffusion models.
LoRAtorio leverages spatially-aware similarity to the base model.
LoRAtorio proposes re-centering the unconditioned score in classifier-free guidance to address domain drift caused by personalization training.
The task of multi-LoRA composition is extended to a dynamic module selection setting in LoRAtorio.
In the dynamic module selection setting, all LoRA adapters are loaded into the base model and selected at inference time based on intrinsic similarity.
LoRAtorio achieves state-of-the-art performance on the ComposLoRA benchmark in terms of automated metrics and human preference.
LoRAtorio's performance is consistent for both static and dynamic module settings.
Evaluation of LoRAtorio is extended to a rectified flow architecture.
The evaluation shows LoRAtorio's robustness.

