Grab-n-Go achieved an average recognition accuracy of 92.0% in a user study with 10 participants and 25 everyday objects.
Grab-n-Go demonstrated robustness against 10 more challenging, deformable objects in a follow-up study.
Grab-n-Go has the potential to provide seamless, unobtrusive interactions without requiring modifications to existing objects.
The complete Grab-n-Go dataset is publicly available.
The Grab-n-Go dataset comprises data from 18 participants performing 30 microgestures with 35 distinct objects.
The Grab-n-Go wristband uses two speaker-microphone pairs.
The speakers on the Grab-n-Go wristband emit acoustic waves in ranges of 18-21 kHz and 21.5-24.5 kHz.
Echo profiles are created by the Grab-n-Go system to infer microgestures.
A customized deep learning model is used by Grab-n-Go for microgesture inference.
Subtle hand gestures, or microgestures, performed while holding objects offer a promising solution for always-available input.
Recognizing microgestures in the presence of objects introduces significant challenges.
Instrumenting objects with sensors can be costly and not feasible for everyday objects.
Wearable-based methods can overcome the limitation of needing object modifications.
Existing wearable solutions typically focus solely on tracking hand movements, ignoring the objects themselves.
Limitations in existing wearable solutions reduce their generalizability across various objects.
Most prior work on microgesture recognition has been restricted to recognizing microgestures across a relatively small set of objects, typically around 10 or fewer.
Active acoustic sensing has potential for tracking fine-grained movements of different body parts.
Previous work has successfully captured fine-grained hand movements and some hand-object interactions.
Previous systems primarily focus on free-hand gestures or the identification of broader object-related activities.
The recognition of hand microgestures performed while holding objects remains an underexplored area within the domain of active acoustic sensing.
Diverse object geometries lead to different grasping poses and signal variations.
Objects in hand can block sensor views, interfering with signal capture.
Grab-n-Go employs active acoustic sensing by using two embedded speakers to emit inaudible acoustic waves toward the hand and the object.
Two microphones on the Grab-n-Go wristband capture reflected waves.
Reflected waves form unique patterns corresponding to each microgesture.
A user study with 10 participants evaluated Grab-n-Go.
The user study examined 5 distinct grasping poses defined by Schlesinger’s grasp taxonomy.
