# Optimal CO2 storage management considering safety constraints in multi-stakeholder multi-site CCS projects: a game theoretic perspective


Jungang Chen
Seyyed A. Hosse

**********Extracted claims**********

Carbon capture and storage (CCS) projects typically involve a diverse array of stakeholders from public, private, and regulatory sectors.
Each stakeholder in CCS projects has different objectives and responsibilities.
CCS projects are often implemented in geologically connected sites.
Shared geological features, such as pressure space and reservoir pore capacity, can lead to competitive behavior among stakeholders in CCS projects.
CO2 storage sites are often located in geologically mature basins that previously served as sites for hydrocarbon extraction or wastewater disposal.
Leveraging existing infrastructures makes unilateral optimization more complicated and unrealistic for CO2 storage sites.
A previously developed surrogate model based on the Embed-to-Control (E2C) framework can address the high computational cost of repeated simulations of high fidelity models.
The effectiveness of the proposed framework in addressing optimal management of CO2 storage is demonstrated when multiple stakeholders with various objectives and goals are involved.
Geological carbon storage (GCS) has emerged as a viable technology in global efforts to mitigate climate change.
GCS permanently sequesters carbon dioxide (CO2) in deep subsurface formations.
GCS plays a critical role in reducing anthropogenic emissions from industrial sources.
GCS enables continued use of fossil-based infrastructure during the energy transition as part of the broader carbon capture and storage (CCS) strategy.
Modern GCS projects are increasingly being implemented at the basin scale.
Basin-scale geological formations span large areas.
Basin-scale geological formations support long-term operations and large-volume CO2 storage.
Large-scale GCS deployments often involve multiple injection sites, complex monitoring and regulatory requirements, and diverse stakeholder participation.
Multi-stakeholder CCS initiatives, where operators, landowners, regulators, and investors interact within geologically connected sites, are becoming increasingly common.
In a multi-stakeholder CCS setting, each stakeholder pursues distinct interests and goals.
Operators typically manage one or more injection/extraction wells.
Operators' objectives can include maximizing CO2 storage or financial returns.
Landowners focus on maximizing land use value and royalty income.
Regulators prioritize long-term safety, pressure containment, and environmental compliance, such as Class VI well regulations.
Only operators physically interact with the reservoir in CCS projects.
Interests of all other stakeholders also shape the operational landscape of CCS projects.
Regulators may impose safety constraints that limit injection volumes and rates in CCS projects.
Landowners and operators may both seek to exploit the available pressure space for maximum economic return in CCS projects.
The interconnected nature of subsurface dynamics means that one operator’s injection strategy can alter reservoir pressure and plume behavior.
Altering reservoir pressure and plume behavior influences the economic and operational outcomes of other operators.
Inter-dependencies in CCS projects pose significant challenges to effective basin-wide coordination.
Without a robust decision-making framework, stakeholders may risk adopting locally optimal strategies that undermine collective performance.
Consequences of undermining collective performance include underutilized reservoir capacity, reduced economic returns, or regulatory noncompliance.
Coordinated multi-agent optimization is essential to balance return, safety, and fairness in basin-scale GCS deployment.
The challenges of optimal management of basin-scale geological carbon storage are multifold, especially when multiple stakeholders with respective goals and interests are involved.
High fidelity models that predict the pressure evolution and plume migration are needed to ensure meeting safety constraints in GCS.
Optimization of a GCS system requires multiple forward evaluations of high fidelity models.
Multiple forward evaluations of GCS models become infeasible when computational resources are limited.
Employing machine learning-based proxy models is a remedy to amortize computational costs in GCS optimization.
The literature on multi-agent optimization for GCS or broader subsurface communities is scarce.
Existing literature either explored multi-objective optimization of one single stakeholder on various CO2 storage projects or constrained co-optimization of one single stakeholder.
Pettersson et al. (2024, 2025) proposed a multi-objective optimization framework that enables coordinated CO2 injection strategies among multiple agents in basin-scale carbon storage projects.
The Pettersson et al. approach facilitates joint optimization.
The Pettersson et al. approach assumes the presence of a single centralized decision maker who assigns operational strategies to all agents.
Solution selection in the Pettersson et al. approach may inherently prioritize certain objectives over others.
The centralized assumption overlooks the reality that, in practice, each agent seeks to maximize its own returns.
The centralized assumption limits the framework’s applicability to competitive multi-operator environments.
Decision-making in competitive multi-operator environments is inherently decentralized and driven by individual objectives.
A framework referred to as constrained Markov game (CMG) is suitable for constrained optimization.
CMG offers real-time optimized solutions when multiple actors are involved.
A constrained Markov game extends the Markov game framework by adding safety constraints.
All agents must comply with safety constraints in a constrained Markov game while optimizing their own objectives.
In a CMG, the system is represented as a sequential decision-making process.
The sequential decision-making process in a CMG is defined by states, actions, transitions, and rewards, along with explicit safety constraints that limit feasible actions.
Safe multi-agent reinforcement learning provides practical methods to learn policies in CMG settings.
Safe multi-agent reinforcement learning ensures agents maximize rewards while avoiding constraint violations.
The CMG framework is particularly suited to multi-stakeholder CCS projects.
Agents in multi-stakeholder CCS projects operate under competing objectives and must comply with safety regulations.
The CMG framework enables the development of policies that are economically optimal.
The CMG framework enables the development of policies that take others’ operational actions into account.
The CMG framework provides a scalable, adaptive decision-making framework for basin-scale CO2 storage management.
This study developed a safe multi-agent reinforcement learning framework tailored for multi-stakeholder, multi-site CO2 storage problems.
The developed framework accommodates divergent interests while ensuring safe operations.
The performance of the developed framework is validated against traditional multi-objective optimization approaches.
A reward and penalty model was meticulously designed for CO2 storage projects in this study.
Various coalition structures based on shared rewards and penalties were investigated to evaluate their impacts on stakeholder objectives and overall system performance in this study.

