# Two-Impulse Trajectory Design in Two-Body Systems    With Riemannian Geometry


Samuel G. Gessow111Ph.D. Student, Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, V

**********Extracted claims**********

A new method for generating impulsive trajectories in restricted two-body systems is presented.
The method leverages Riemannian geometry.
The proposed method transforms the standard trajectory optimization problem into a purely geometric one.
The geometric transformation involves computing a set of geodesics for a suitable Riemannian metric.
The Jacobi metric is specifically used to embed dynamics directly into the metric.
Any geodesic of the defined metric is a dynamically feasible trajectory.
The method finds fuel-optimal transfer trajectories by sampling candidate energy changes for different points on the current and desired orbit.
The method efficiently computes and evaluates each candidate geodesic.
Candidate geodesics are equivalent to candidate orbit transfer trajectories via the Jacobi metric.
The method bypasses known issues of optimization-based methods.
The method is not sensitive to the initial guess.
The method can be applied to more complex two-body systems.
