Many existing methods cannot handle the J2 perturbation case.
Numerical simulations and performance comparisons showcase the effectiveness of the approach.
Finding fuel-optimal orbit transfer trajectories is a fundamental problem in space mission planning.
The problem of finding fuel-optimal orbit transfer trajectories is often expressed as finding a maneuver that minimizes the overall change in velocity (ΔV) required by a spacecraft.
In classical trajectory design, a spacecraft is assumed to generate instantaneous ΔV through an impulsive maneuver.
This assumption reduces the orbit transfer problem to determining when, where, and by how much ΔV needs to change.
Modern trajectory design methods are focused on continuous thrust maneuvers.
Impulsive trajectories are still used to provide a reasonable initial guess for numerical solvers.
Some of the first works on impulsive trajectory design date back more than a century.
Algorithms for designing impulsive trajectories often rely on numerical methods.
These numerical methods are sensitive to the quality of the initial guess.
These numerical methods can converge to local minima.
These numerical methods can diverge completely.
The new impulsive trajectory design framework reformulates the trajectory design problem as a purely geometric curve finding problem.
The new framework bypasses sensitivities observed in other methods.
The new framework generalizes to more challenging or complex problems.
The new framework can generalize to non-spherical bodies.
There has been a growing interest and success in applying geometric concepts to trajectory planning and control.
New methods are being developed using geodesics to represent dynamically feasible trajectories.
Geodesics are defined as the shortest path between two points on a manifold in Riemannian geometry.
Geodesics are the shortest curve connecting any two points on a manifold given a distance function (metric).
If trajectories can be converted to geodesics, efficient solvers for computing geodesics can be employed.
These solvers bypass issues with numerical optimization in the time domain.
To convert a trajectory planning problem into a geodesic computation problem, a metric must be found that naturally embeds the dynamics.
Any geodesic of such a metric is also a dynamically feasible trajectory.
Certain classes of systems have known metrics whose geodesics describe state trajectories.
Lagrangian systems with an accompanying Jacobi metric are relevant to this work.
The connection between geometry and dynamically feasible trajectories is utilized in this work.
This connection is used to design impulsive spacecraft trajectories that minimize a performance index.
A new geometric framework for computing minimum-ΔV two-impulse phase-free orbital transfers is a main contribution of this paper.
The framework generalizes easily to more complex gravity models.
The framework avoids local minima.
