This formulation enables efficient computation.
This formulation circumvents convergence issues common in traditional methods.
Spacecraft operating in a restricted two-body system possess the Jacobi metric.
Geodesics of the Jacobi metric are dynamically feasible trajectories for spacecraft in a restricted two-body system.
Combining the insight about the Jacobi metric with a sampled-based planning algorithm allows efficient computation of fuel-optimal transfer trajectories.
This efficient computation is possible even for more complex restricted two-body problems, such as those with a modeled J2 perturbation.
Prior work has addressed J2-perturbed transfers through iterative corrections.
The presented framework directly incorporates gravitational perturbations such as J2 into the metric itself.
The presented framework does not rely on precomputed unperturbed solutions.
The approach lays the groundwork for extending geometric methods to increasingly complex mission environments.

