Cu Cui222Interdisciplinary Center for Scientific Computing (IWR), Faculty for Engineering Sciences, Heide

**********Extracted claims**********

A multigrid method for an unfitted finite element discretization of the Dirichlet boundary value problem is presented.
The discretization employs Nitsche’s method to implement the boundary condition.
The discretization uses additional face-based ghost penalties for stabilization.
Standard intergrid operators are applied.
The relevant domain of computation does not grow under mesh refinement.
The smoother is a parallel implementation of the multiplicative vertex-patch smoother.
The multiplicative vertex-patch smoother uses inconsistent treatment of ghost penalties.
Computational results indicate that a fast converging method is obtained.
Runtime comparisons to fitted methods show moderate losses.
Many optimizations for Cartesian vertex patches cannot be applied on cut patches.
The article's goal is to combine the advantages of unfitted finite element discretizations with state-of-the-art GPU implementations of finite element solvers.
Unfitted finite element methods provide a powerful framework for handling complex geometries.
Unfitted finite element methods provide a powerful framework for handling moving boundaries and interfaces.
Unfitted finite element methods enable the use of computationally efficient tensor techniques in the bulk of the domain.
A geometric multigrid technique based on vertex-patch smoothers is established to obtain fast solvers for such discretizations.
A geometric multigrid technique based on vertex-patch smoothers is established to obtain efficient preconditioners for such discretizations.
The realization of unfitted finite elements focuses on the CutFEM method.
Ghost penalties are relied upon for handling instabilities due to small cuts.
Prior work presented a multigrid method for the unfitted interface problem.
In prior work, a Gauss-Seidel smoother is used inside two subdomains.
In prior work, the Gauss-Seidel smoother is combined with a global solver for degrees of freedom coupling over the interface.
Prior methods obtain good iteration counts independent of contrast.
Prior methods can be expected to work for the unfitted boundary value problem.
A global solver for interface degrees of freedom necessitates data structures which impede the flow of information on the GPU.
A thesis presents a basis change at the interface that renders good convergence rates without a global solution involving the interface matrix.
Results for a pp-multigrid method for higher order discretizations are presented in a thesis.
The thesis employs face-patch solvers for pp-multigrid methods.
Prior work presents and analyzes a domain decomposition preconditioner for the CutFEM interface problem.
Estimates for a domain decomposition preconditioner in prior work are not robust.
Prior work presents computational results for the Dirichlet boundary value problem.
A method related to the presented work was developed for unfitted isogeometric methods.
In the related method, a cell-based additive Schwarz method for all cut cells is combined with a Jacobi method on uncut cells.
The related method exhibits the usual dependence of the condition number for second order problems, specifically κ∼h−2.
The condition number in the related method is independent of the size and location of the cuts.
The vertex-patch smoother was introduced by Pavarino as a particular implementation of a multilevel domain decomposition method.
The vertex-patch smoother, in its additive and multiplicative form, has proven to be a robust smoother for many applications.
The vertex-patch smoother can be implemented in an efficient way.
The vertex-patch smoother provides the computational intensity needed to exploit modern computers.
The vertex-patch smoother enables implementations on GPU with high utilization.
High utilization on GPU is exemplified by CUDA and Tensor cores of the Nvidia A100.
Numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves robustness in GPU implementations for unfitted methods.
Numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach delivers performance comparable to that observed on standard Cartesian geometries.
The described advancement is a significant step toward extending GPU-accelerated solvers to complex geometries and unfitted methods.
The described advancement underscores the potential for combining multigrid techniques with Schwarz smoothers to efficiently manage the computational demands of CutFEM on modern hardware.

