# CoreEditor: Consistent 3D Editing via Correspondence-constrained Diffusion


Zhe Zhu, Honghua Chen, Peng Li, and Mingqiang Wei

Z. Zhu, P. Li, and M. Wei are with the 

**********Extracted claims**********

Text-driven 3D editing aims to modify 3D scenes based on textual descriptions.
Most existing text-driven 3D editing approaches adapt pre-trained 2D image editors to multi-view inputs.
Existing approaches often fail to maintain cross-view consistency without explicit control over multi-view information exchange.
This failure in existing approaches leads to insufficient edits and blurry details.
CoreEditor is a novel framework for consistent text-to-3D editing.
The key innovation of CoreEditor is a correspondence-constrained attention mechanism.
This mechanism enforces precise interactions between pixels that are expected to remain consistent throughout the diffusion denoising process.
CoreEditor incorporates semantic similarity estimated during denoising.
The incorporation of semantic similarity enables more reliable correspondence modeling and robust multi-view editing beyond sole reliance on geometric alignment.
CoreEditor includes a selective editing pipeline.
This pipeline allows users to choose preferred results from multiple candidates.
The selective editing pipeline offers greater flexibility and user control.
Neural 3D representations, such as NeRF and Gaussian Splatting, have advanced photorealistic novel view synthesis.
These representations have established new benchmarks for fidelity and efficiency.
These methods excel at reconstructing high-quality 3D scenes from multi-view images.
Modifying constructed 3D scenes to align with user preferences remains a challenging task.
Developing a 3D editing tool has become a critical research focus.
Most text-driven 3D editing approaches adapt diffusion-based text-to-image (T2I) models for zero-shot editing of multi-view images.
The stochastic sampling process of diffusion models often induces cross-view inconsistencies.
Cross-view inconsistencies undermine the reliability of diffusion models for 3D scene optimization.
InstructNeRF2NeRF addresses cross-view inconsistency through an iterative dataset update scheme.
InstructNeRF2NeRF suffers from slow convergence.
Subsequent works accelerate the editing process and enhance quality by jointly editing multiple views.
These subsequent works enforce inter-view alignment using strategies like depth-conditioned ControlNet, cross-frame attention, and cross-view feature interpolation.
These strategies lack explicit constraints on multi-view information exchange.
This lack of constraints compromises the consistency of image details.
This limitation is particularly pronounced under significant viewpoint variations and complex occlusions.
The limitation often leads to inadequate edits and degraded details.
GaussCtrl produces inconsistent and low-quality multi-view edits.
Inconsistent and low-quality multi-view edits from GaussCtrl result in rendering with blurry texture.
CoreEditor achieves 3D consistency by integrating precise multi-view constraints into a pre-trained T2I diffusion model.
Tokens from different images can collaborate within the attention module of a T2I model while generating reasonable results.
CoreEditor introduces a Correspondence-constrained Attention (CCA) mechanism in the diffusion U-Net.
In CCA, image patches rendered from the same 3D point are constrained to interact with one another.
This interaction aims to improve visual consistency.
The revised information flow direction of the attention module can significantly improve consistency between multi-view generated content.
This improvement occurs without fine-tuning or re-training the diffusion model.
When camera distances between views are large, background image patches may have few geometrically corresponding patches in other views due to occlusion.
An insufficient token count causes the attention process to become highly unstable.
This instability often leads to low-quality and over-saturated outputs.
When significant disparity exists among per-view editing results, CCA tends to "average" these edits.
This averaging results in unnatural edits.
CoreEditor designs a geometry and semantic co-supported approach to extract multi-view correspondences.
Semantically similar patches can be involved in attention to improve consistency.
In regions where geometric correspondences are unavailable, additional correspondences are calculated based on diffusion feature similarity.
This comprehensive correspondence enables CCA to generate more plausible multi-view edits.
CoreEditor addresses the second problem with its selective editing pipeline.
Users can select a preferred editing pattern from per-view editing results within the selective editing pipeline.
The selected edit is injected into the diffusion model by a Reference Attention (RA).
This approach ensures preliminary alignment of the global editing style.
This enables CCA to focus solely on local consistency.
CoreEditor can generate diverse yet faithful 3D edits by selecting different per-view editing patterns.
CoreEditor achieves superior results compared to competitors in terms of multi-view consistency and editing quality.
CoreEditor significantly improves multi-view consistency through its Correspondence-constrained Attention mechanism.
The geometry and semantic co-supported approach for building multi-view correspondences significantly improves editing quality in complex scenes.
The selective editing pipeline allows for a flexible and user-centered 3D editing experience.

