The sensors need to be lightweight and have a small form factor for the SCA mounting.
This work builds upon the baseline established in Walt and Krishnan (2023).
The baseline study employed Inertial Measurement Units (IMUs) and an infrared (IR) reflectance sensor to classify grasp states in an agricultural context.
The baseline study demonstrated notable success in detecting slip events.
The baseline study faced challenges in identifying other critical transitions, such as from slip to a successful pick.
The present work investigates a broader range of sensing modalities and modeling techniques.
The present work aims to enable more comprehensive and robust grasp state classification.
This work adds three sensors: a tactile sensor, a custom tension sensor, and a tip RGB camera.
The tactile and tension sensors were added with the intention of addressing challenges in differentiating between no slip and successful pick.
The camera was added because it is a powerful sensor that can play many roles in manipulation.
All three added sensors (tactile, tension, RGB camera) have been found effective in slip detection in a non-agricultural setting.
A classifier (C) was created such that for a given set of sensor data xt at time t, it returns a classification Y.
The possible classifications Y are: {slip, no slip, successful pick, failed grasp}.
n represents the number of features (sensor data).
m represents the window size of the data.
