The Random Forest classifier is an ensemble method.
The Random Forest classifier uses multiple decision trees created with different subsets of the input data.
The output classification of the Random Forest is the consensus of the classifications of each tree.
The input into the Random Forest is a 25 sample window.
The 25 sample window for Random Forest is due to the use of a Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) being applied to the IMU data.
The LSTM is a recursive neural network.
The LSTM is designed to work with time sequence data.
The LSTM is designed to capture longer term dependencies.
The LSTM uses a single time sample window.
Tests with a Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) did not produce strong results for the LSTM.
Random Forest and LSTM methods were selected due to their demonstrated effectiveness in grasp state classification.
The Random Forest is very effective at multi-class classification problems.
The LSTM is good at time series data.
This work seeks to further develop methods and sensors for enhancing agricultural manipulation through grasp state estimation.
An improved model and sensor suite can perform grasp state classification tailored to the agricultural setting.
The improved model and sensor suite addresses failings seen in earlier works.
A model was trained in the lab.
The model was successfully transferred to real plants.
The work provides an understanding of the roles that sensors play in agricultural grasp state classification.
The work provides an objective comparison of two distinct classification models.
This work focuses on identifying the minimum suite of sensors and associated machine learning classification algorithms that can robustly classify all grasp states.

