# Aware First, Think Less: Dynamic Boundary Self-Awareness Drives Extreme Reasoning Efficiency in Large Language Models


Qiguang Chen1∗  Dengyun Peng1∗  Jinhao Liu1  HuiKang Su1  Jiannan Guan1  Lib

**********Extracted claims**********

Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have significantly improved their capabilities on complex reasoning tasks through Long Chain-of-Thought (CoT).
The Long Chain-of-Thought (CoT) approach often results in substantial redundancy.
The redundancy from Long CoT impairs computational efficiency.
The redundancy from Long CoT causes significant delays in real-time applications.
Current methods to improve efficiency often rely on human-defined difficulty priors.
Human-defined difficulty priors do not align with an LLM’s self-awared difficulty.
The misalignment between human-defined priors and LLM self-awareness leads to inefficiencies.
The Dynamic Reasoning-Boundary Self-Awareness Framework (DR. SAF) enables models to dynamically assess and adjust their reasoning depth in response to problem complexity.
DR. SAF integrates three key components: Boundary Self-Awareness Alignment, Adaptive Reward Management, and a Boundary Preservation Mechanism.
The components of DR. SAF allow models to optimize their reasoning processes.
DR. SAF balances efficiency and accuracy without compromising performance.
Experimental results demonstrate that DR. SAF achieves a 49.27% reduction in total response tokens with minimal loss in accuracy.
DR. SAF delivers a 6.59x gain in token efficiency.
DR. SAF delivers a 5x reduction in training time.
DR. SAF is well-suited to resource-limited settings.
During extreme training, DR. SAF can surpass traditional instruction-based models in token efficiency.
DR. SAF can achieve more than 16% accuracy improvement during extreme training compared to traditional instruction-based models.
Long Chain-of-Thought (Long CoT) involves a more detailed and progressive process of exploration and reflection based on a given problem.
Long CoT is facilitated by inference-time scaling.
Long CoT has significantly advanced areas such as mathematical and logical reasoning.
Long CoT has provided new insights into the role of supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement learning (RL) in enhancing the availability of extended reasoning chains.
The Long CoT paradigm generates substantial redundant tokens.
The redundant tokens from Long CoT significantly impair computational efficiency.
The redundant tokens from Long CoT lead to unacceptable application latency.
Several approaches focus on optimizing reasoning length to mitigate the issue of redundancy.
Some studies incorporate compression techniques like static pruning thresholds to filter out intermediate tokens.
Some studies incorporate adaptive routing to more efficient modules.
Other approaches enhance a model’s inherent ability to produce concise reasoning paths through specific techniques.
AdaptThink and DAST propose frameworks that dynamically adjust models’ reasoning depth based on predefined measures of problem complexity.
Huang et al. extended paradigms for dynamically adjusting reasoning depth to human-designed adaptive budgeting.
Current methods often rely on manually designed static priors of difficulty and target length.
Current methods neglect each LLM’s intrinsically evolving reasoning boundaries during training.
Problems initially identified as “simple” may remain challenging for an LLM whose capability still needs longer exploration.
Problems previously judged as “complex” might later be handled intuitively by the model through shorter reasoning processes.
The reliance on static priors leads to inefficient reasoning processes and suboptimal performance.
DR. SAF assesses problem difficulty relative to a model’s reasoning capacity.
Boundary Self-Awareness Alignment enables LLMs to recognize their real-time reasoning boundaries.
Self-awareness allows the model to assess the difficulty of a given question based on its own capabilities.
Self-awareness prompts self-guided adjustments in reasoning depth and answer length.
Adaptive Length Management refines efficiency by adapting the reward according to the model’s real-time boundaries.
Adaptive Length Management encourages longer exploration beyond the Completely Infeasible Reasoning Boundary (CIRB).
Adaptive Length Management encourages shorter reasoning within the Completely Feasible Reasoning Boundary (CFRB).
Adaptive Length Management ensures that the model does not oversimplify and compromise quality.
The Boundary Preservation Mechanism maintains stability by preventing the collapse of real-time reasoning boundaries during training.
The Boundary Preservation Mechanism ensures that all correct responses receive non-negative reinforcement.
DR. SAF addresses the traditional trade-off issue between efficiency and accuracy.
DR. SAF enables models to dynamically adjust their reasoning depth based on their capabilities.
DR. SAF enhances a model’s boundary self-awareness.
DR. SAF enforces boundary-driven length adaptation.
DR. SAF preserves real-time reasoning boundaries.
DR. SAF enables real-time control of reasoning depth without degrading performance.
When evaluated on six public benchmarks, applying DR. SAF to the distilled Qwen-2.5 model reduces total response tokens by 49.27%.
Applying DR. SAF to the distilled Qwen-2.5 model achieves state-of-the-art token efficiency.
DR. SAF delivers a 6.59x gain in token efficiency compared with the distilled model.
After additional continual training, the extremely compressed DR. SAF model can surpass traditional instruction-based models in token efficiency.
After additional continual training, the extremely compressed DR. SAF model can increase accuracy by more than 16% compared to traditional instruction-based models.
On the distilled Qwen-3 model, DR. SAF reduces training steps by 80% compared with previous reinforcement-learning-based methods.
The 80% reduction in training steps makes DR. SAF attractive for deployments with limited computational resources.
Existing efficient reasoning methods often rely on human-annotated difficulty priors that do not align with LLMs’ reasoning requirements.
The misalignment between human-annotated difficulty priors and LLM reasoning requirements leads to inefficient reasoning processes and suboptimal performance.
The DR. SAF framework enables models to dynamically assess their own reasoning boundaries.
The DR. SAF framework adaptively manages length reward signals based on problem feasibility.
The DR. SAF framework prevents models from reasoning boundary collapse.
DR. SAF demonstrates substantial improvements in efficiency across 6 benchmarks.

