Content: # Causality Matters: How Temporal Information    Emerges in Video Language Models


Yumeng Shi,
Quanyu Long,
Yin Wu,
Wenya Wang


###### Abstract


Video language models (VideoLMs) have made significant progress in multimodal understanding. However, tempo

**********Extracted claims**********

Video language models have achieved significant progress in a wide range of video understanding tasks, including captioning, question answering, and temporal reasoning.
Temporal understanding remains a core challenge in video language models.
Temporal understanding is defined as the ability to recognize and interpret the order, duration, and relationships between events over time.
Temporal understanding stands out as both fundamental and difficult among the various challenges posed by video understanding tasks.
Temporal understanding plays a vital role in enabling intelligent video-language interactions.
Prior works emphasize positional encodings (PEs) as a key mechanism for encoding temporal structure.
It is commonly assumed that using a more sophisticated positional encoding function leads directly to improved temporal awareness.
This belief has spurred a variety of modifications to positional encodings.
Modifications to positional encodings include extending them to higher dimensions.
Modifications to positional encodings include designing variable-rate formats.
Removing or modifying positional encodings in video inputs yields minimal degradation in the performance of temporal understanding.
Positional encodings have only a marginal impact on temporal understanding performance beyond the first layer.
Query positional encodings play a more significant role in temporal understanding.
Video input positional encodings contribute little to temporal understanding.
Reversing the frame sequence while preserving original positional encodings causes a substantial drop in performance.
Video language models are highly sensitive to the order of video frames.
Video language models do not primarily rely on explicit positional encodings to detect frame order.
Positional encodings are not the primary source of temporal information currently.
Temporal information emerges from the causal attention mechanism’s order-aware structure.
Causal attention implicitly encodes temporal structure.
Temporal information is progressively constructed via a multi-stage causal pathway.
Temporal cues are progressively synthesized through inter-frame attention.
Temporal information emerges from long-range inter-video interactions.
Raw information is first aggregated across video frames in the model’s early and middle layers.
Temporal information is aggregated in the final video frame.
Temporal information flows into the query through the final video frame.
Temporal cues are subsequently integrated into the query tokens.
The model generates its answer primarily from the query, rather than directly referencing video evidence at the final stage.
The resulting representation is then used to enrich the query’s context.
Temporal information is then processed independently by the query without further contribution from the video input.
The entire information pathway is genuinely causal.
The tracing provides direct evidence of how temporal understanding emerges within the model.
Experiments were conducted with Qwen2.5-VL and LLaVA-OneVision models.
Qwen2.5-VL and LLaVA-OneVision represent state-of-the-art VideoLMs.
Staged cross-modal attention is an efficiency-oriented strategy.
Staged Modality Interaction for Sparse Attention reduces cross-modal interaction to improve computational efficiency.
A temporal exit mechanism for early token truncation is an efficiency-oriented strategy.
Temporal Exit for KV Cache Compression alleviates GPU memory pressure by discarding tokens that no longer contribute to temporal information propagation.
Experiments on two benchmarks validate the effectiveness of both proposed approaches.
The presented work systematically investigates video temporal understanding in VideoLMs.
The presented work offers insights for future model improvement.

