# Activate Me!: Designing Efficient Activation Functions for Privacy-Preserving Machine Learning with Fully Homomorphic Encryption


Nges Brian Njungle and Mich

**********Extracted claims**********

Machine learning integration into applications in sensitive domains like healthcare and defense raises privacy and security concerns.
Applications in sensitive domains require strong privacy protections because they rely on large amounts of sensitive data for both training and inference.
Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) allows computations on encrypted data.
FHE preserves confidentiality throughout the entire machine learning pipeline.
FHE natively supports only linear operations.
Implementing non-linear activation functions under FHE constraints poses challenges.
Non-linear activation functions are crucial for modern machine learning applications.
The study designed, implemented, and evaluated activation functions optimized for FHE-based machine learning applications.
The study focuses on the Square function and the Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU).
The Square function and Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU) are widely used functions.
Experiments utilized the LeNet-5 and ResNet-20 architectures.
The CKKS scheme from the OpenFHE library was used for implementing the architectures.
Two approaches for ReLU were compared in the study.
A low-degree polynomial approximation approach for ReLU was explored.
A novel scheme-switching technique that securely evaluates ReLU under FHE constraints was introduced.
The square function is highly effective in shallow models such as LeNet-5.
The square function achieves 99.4% accuracy in LeNet-5 models.
The square function has an inference time of 128 seconds per image in LeNet-5 models.
ReLU is more appropriate in deeper networks like ResNet-20.
An FHE-based ResNet-20 model implemented with ReLU polynomial approximation resulted in an accuracy of 83.8%.
The FHE-based ResNet-20 model with ReLU polynomial approximation had an inference time of 1,145 seconds per image.
The proposed scheme-switching algorithm achieved an accuracy of 89.8% on a ResNet-20 model.
The proposed scheme-switching algorithm had an inference time of 1,697 seconds per image on a ResNet-20 model.
Results highlight a key trade-off in selecting activation functions for FHE-based machine learning applications.
Activation functions that reduce computation time tend to significantly lower accuracy.
Activation functions that preserve accuracy incur higher computational costs.
Machine learning (ML) has emerged as a cornerstone of Artificial Intelligence (AI).
Machine learning is often regarded as a key driver for the Fourth Industrial Revolution.
Machine learning has experienced unprecedented growth over the past decade.
Machine learning has become an essential asset to technology across various sectors, including healthcare, finance, and defense.
The ability of ML to derive insights from vast datasets has proven invaluable in critical systems.
Examples of critical systems include medical diagnostics, predictive healthcare, and military decision making.
In defense, ML is used in high-stakes applications such as autonomous weapon systems, threat detection, cybersecurity, and intelligence analysis.
These ML capabilities enable faster, data-driven decision making.
These ML capabilities significantly enhance operational efficiency and mission effectiveness.
The successful deployment of ML in critical applications depends highly on access to large volumes of sensitive data for both training and inference.
The dependency on sensitive data presents serious privacy and security challenges.
Data privacy and security are particularly acute in military applications.
Data involved in military applications are often classified or highly confidential.
Unauthorized access, leakage, or misuse of military data could compromise national security, operational plans, or strategic assets.
Ensuring data security and privacy in ML workflows in military systems is a technical necessity.
Ensuring data security and privacy in ML workflows in military systems is a matter of national security.
Traditional cryptographic methods are effective at securing data during storage or transmission.
Traditional cryptographic methods fall short at protecting data during processing.
Data must be decrypted before processing in most cases.
Decrypting data before processing can expose it to unauthorized access.
Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) has emerged as a promising solution to the drawback of traditional cryptography.
FHE ensures privacy and security are maintained throughout the entire data life cycle.
FHE is particularly valuable for privacy-preserving machine learning (PPML) applications.
Data security and privacy are paramount in PPML applications.
The application of FHE in ML models presents two key challenges: a very high computational cost of evaluating models and the inability to efficiently evaluate non-linear functions.
Artificial neural networks are the dominant sub-field of ML.
Artificial neural networks depend on non-linear activation functions to introduce complexity and enable effective learning.
Current FHE schemes typically support only linear operations.
The limitation to linear operations severely restricts the size and practical application of artificial neural networks under FHE constraints.
Prior research on ML and FHE focused on enhancing computational efficiency through optimizations in FHE-based model design.
The study focuses on the design and implementation of activation functions tailored for FHE-based ML models.
The study examines two commonly used activation functions in FHE-based neural network architectures: the square function and the Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU).
Early FHE-compatible models predominantly employed simple polynomial activations.
The square function was the most widely used in early FHE-compatible models.
Recent advancements have shifted FHE-based models toward adopting polynomial approximations of the ReLU activation function.
The approximation of ReLU approach resulted in performance improvements.
Little systematic investigation exists regarding contexts and model architectures where different activation functions perform best under FHE constraints.
The study bridges a gap by implementing FHE-based neural networks using various activation functions and rigorously evaluating their effectiveness.
The performance of different activation functions was evaluated using the LeNet-5 and ResNet-20 architectures.
LeNet-5 represents shallow artificial neural network models.
ResNet-20 represents deep artificial neural network models.
The widely adopted polynomial approximation technique was implemented for the non-linear ReLU activation function.
A novel algorithm leveraging FHE scheme-switching capabilities was introduced.
This novel algorithm enables a high-precision evaluation of ReLU under FHE constraints.
The approaches are compared based on inference latency and accuracy.
The comparison emphasizes trade-offs in designing activation functions for FHE-based ML models.
Results indicate that the square function is highly effective for shallow networks such as LeNet-5.
ReLU is more suitable for deeper architectures such as ResNet-20.
The polynomial approximation-based approach delivered a relatively low inference latency.
The polynomial approximation-based approach introduced a higher accuracy degradation.
The scheme-switching method achieved a higher model accuracy with minimal degradation.
The scheme-switching method incurred increased computational overhead.
These findings provide insights into the design of FHE-compatible activation functions.
These findings contribute to the development of more practical and efficient PPML models and solutions.
Activation functions for FHE-based ML models, specifically the square and ReLU, were designed and implemented as a contribution of this work.
A novel algorithm using FHE's scheme-switching capabilities was introduced to evaluate the ReLU activation function in the encrypted domain as a contribution of this work.
A comprehensive evaluation was conducted for the square function, the polynomial approximation of ReLU, and the proposed scheme-switching ReLU method.
LeNet-5 and ResNet-20 models were used for this evaluation under FHE constraints.
The trade-offs of these approaches in terms of model accuracy and inference latency were highlighted.

