Title: Intelligent Edge Resource Provisioning for Scalable Digital Twins of Autonomous Vehicles
URL: https://arxiv.org/html/2508.11574v1
Content: License: CC BY 4.0)

arXiv:2508.11574v1  15 Aug 2025

# Intelligent Edge Resource Provisioning for   Scalable Digital Twins of Autonomous Vehicles


Mohammad Sajid Shahriar1,
Suresh Subramaniam2,
Motoharu Matsuura3,

Hiroshi Hasegawa4, and Shih-Chun Lin1


**********Extracted claims**********

Next-generation networks offer significant potential to advance Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) through the integration of Digital Twins (DTs).
Ensuring the uninterrupted operation of Digital Twins (DTs) through efficient computing resource management remains an open challenge.
A distributed computing architecture that integrates Digital Twins (DTs) and Mobile Edge Computing (MEC) within a software-defined vehicular networking framework is introduced in this paper.
The introduced architecture enables intelligent, low-latency transportation services.
A network-aware scalable collaborative task provisioning algorithm is developed to train an autonomous agent.
The developed algorithm is evaluated using a realistic connected autonomous vehicle (CAV) traffic simulation.
The proposed framework significantly enhances the robustness and scalability of Digital Twin (DT) operations.
The proposed framework reduces synchronization errors to as low as 5%.
The proposed framework achieves up to 99.5% utilization of edge computing resources.
Emerging 6G networks are expected to greatly improve Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS).
6G networks are expected to offer faster data exchange, lower delays, and more stable connections.
A Digital Twin (DT) is a real-time, virtual model of a physical system.
A Digital Twin (DT) continuously collects and processes data from the real world.
Digital Twins (DTs) enable the analysis and prediction of system behavior without interfering with the physical environment.
In complex and rapidly evolving scenarios such as the Internet of Vehicles (IoV), Digital Twins (DTs) offer an efficient and cost-effective means of monitoring, testing, and optimizing system performance.
When integrated with data-intensive and large-scale sensor sources from autonomous vehicles (e.g., cameras, LiDAR, radar), digital twins provide a powerful platform.
Digital twins support developing and validating artificial intelligence (AI) models.
Digital twins support evaluating traffic safety strategies.
Digital twins support facilitating cooperative decision-making between vehicles and infrastructure without impacting real-world traffic operations.
Supporting data-driven applications requires ultra-low latency and fast response times.
Mobile Edge Computing (MEC) brings computational resources closer to vehicles and roadside units.
Mobile Edge Computing (MEC) minimizes delays caused by communication with distant cloud servers.
Managing the increasing complexity and scale of edge-based networking systems remains a significant challenge.
Software-Defined Networking (SDN) offers a more adaptable and centralized approach to network management.
Software-Defined Networking (SDN) decouples the data forwarding layer from the control layer.
Software-Defined Networking (SDN) leverages intelligent controllers or computing devices that dynamically manage network behavior and resource allocation.
A key research challenge involves integrating and coordinating Digital Twins (DTs), Mobile Edge Computing (MEC), and Software-Defined Networking (SDN) within a unified computing framework.
A distributed computing architecture is proposed that integrates edge computing capabilities within a software-defined vehicular networking framework.
The proposed integrated infrastructure combines communication and computation.
The proposed integrated infrastructure enables a novel and dynamic resource provisioning method for data-driven services with ultra-low latency.
A two-tier architecture for Digital Twins (DTs) is proposed.
In the proposed two-tier architecture, data-intensive processing is carried out in a distributed manner.
The proposed two-tier architecture ensures efficient control of data transmission within the same computing domain.
The synchronization of Digital Twins (DTs) with their connected autonomous vehicle (CAV) counterparts is modeled.
Collaborative task computation using an edge network is modeled to achieve efficient Digital Twin (DT) synchronization.
A scalable framework is developed that includes a Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) algorithm to train an autonomous agent.
