# Nominal Evaluation of Automatic Multi-Sections Control Potential in Comparison To   A Simpler One- or Two-Sections Alternative With Predictive Spray Switching


Mogens Plessen


###### Abstract

**********Extracted claims**********

Automatic Section Control (ASC) is a long-standing trend for spraying in agriculture.
Automatic Section Control (ASC) promises to minimize spray overlap areas.
The core idea of Automatic Section Control (ASC) is to switch off spray nozzles on areas that have already been sprayed.
The core idea of Automatic Section Control (ASC) also involves dynamically adjusting nozzle flow rates along the boom bar when velocities of boom sections vary during turn maneuvers.
Automatic Section Control (ASC) requires sensors, particularly for accurate positioning data.
Spraying and the movement of modern wide boom bars are highly dynamic processes.
Many uncertainty factors, such as cross wind drift, boom height, and nozzle clogging in open-field conditions, affect the spraying process.
An Automatic Multi-Sections Control method is compared to a proposed simpler one- or two-sections alternative.
The proposed simpler alternative uses predictive spray switching.
The comparison of methods is provided under nominal conditions.
Agricultural spraying is intrinsically linked to area coverage path planning and spray switching logic.
Combinations of two area coverage path planning and switching logics are compared.
Three sections-setups are compared.
The three sections-setups control either 48 sections, 2 sections, or all nozzles uniformly with the same control signal as one single section.
Methods are evaluated on 10 diverse real-world field examples.
The real-world field examples include non-convex field contours, freeform mainfield lanes, and multiple obstacle areas.
A preferred method is suggested.
The suggested preferred method minimizes area coverage pathlength.
The suggested preferred method offers intermediate overlap.
The suggested preferred method is suitable for manual driving by following a pre-planned predictive spray switching logic for an area coverage path plan.
The suggested preferred method can be implemented sensor-free and at low cost, in contrast to Automatic Section Control (ASC).
Spraying applications play a major role in open-space cereal crop cultivation, including grains like wheat, rapeseed, and barley.
Spraying applications include the spraying of herbicides, pesticides, or similar substances for plant protection.
Spraying applications can also refer to the spraying of fertilizing means.
Spraying applications generally refer to applications where input means are sprayed onto a work area through a set of nozzles.
Multiple field runs are required throughout a crop cycle to apply different sprays.
Spraying in open-space agriculture is typically realized by moving machinery, such as ground-based vehicles and aerial vehicles.
Moving machinery in open-space agriculture follows an area coverage path plan while spray is simultaneously applied to crops through nozzles.
Nozzles are attached to a boom bar aligned along a working width.
The boom bar is carried by the machinery for applying spray along the working width.
Uniform spray application over the entire field area is typically desired.
Alternative scenarios, such as spot spraying or banding, exist where only specific spots or bands of the field area are sprayed.
The paper's topic is uniform spraying.
An area coverage path plan is desired for uniform spraying that avoids area coverage gaps and overlaps.
An area coverage path plan is desired for uniform spraying that minimizes area coverage pathlength.
An area coverage path plan for uniform spraying is subject to the constraint of a given working width.
Agricultural spraying is also intrinsically linked to the development of logics for spray switching (on and off switching of nozzles).
A second strategy for efficient spraying and resource saving involves suitably switching individual nozzles on and off for areas already sprayed.
This second strategy also involves varying nozzle flow rates of individual nozzles along turn maneuvers to compensate for different traveling velocities of different boom bar sections.
This second strategy is named Automatic Section Control (ASC) or Automatic Boom Section Control.
The research in this paper is motivated by the observed complexity of the spraying process and Automatic Section Control (ASC) in open-space agriculture.
The spraying process is affected by a remarkable number of parameters.
These parameters include nozzle type, spray fan angle, spray pressure, boom height, nozzle spray overlap, nozzle spacing, nozzle clogging, machinery traveling speed changes, and cross wind for spray drift.
Dynamic effects are further amplified in unmanned aerial spraying compared to traditional spraying with tractors or ground-based machinery.
For Automatic Section Control (ASC), accurate localization data and velocity estimates are required along a wide boom bar.
A wide boom bar's movement is highly dynamic.
A wide boom bar's movement is affected by mechanical structure vibrations and inertia.
Gaps between green areas in an illustration of map-based Automatic Section Control are due to discretely sampled boom section states.
Multiple overlapping green areas can be observed in an illustration of map-based Automatic Section Control.
The discrete nature of displayed results in an illustration implies that delays between switching changes naturally occur.
Adjacent path segments (mainfield lanes) in an illustration do not have a constant distance to each other.
The non-constant distance between adjacent path segments in an illustration results in excessive overlapping potential.
An illustration visualizes four typical difficulties associated with Automatic Section Control (ASC).
The paper focuses its analysis on nominal conditions.
The paper aims to determine the best-case performance an optimal Automatic Section Control (ASC) solution can achieve under nominal conditions compared to an alternative practical method.
The paper's analysis searches for a performance upper bound of Automatic Section Control (ASC) under nominal conditions.
Real-world savings potential is degraded by uncertainty factors.
An alternative practical method is suggested for comparison.
The suggested alternative practical method differs from a common Automatic Section Control (ASC) solution in two independent aspects.
The predictive spray switching method is employed as part of the suggested alternative.
The predictive spray switching method is extended to handle non-convex field areas and multiple obstacle areas, such as tree islands, ponds, or power pole masts inside the field area.
The predictive spray switching method uses a predictive switching logic tailored to a specific path pattern.
This predictive switching logic contrasts with the reactive switching logic common for Automatic Section Control (ASC).
Automatic Section Control (ASC) commonly uses reactive switching logic based on Boustrophedon-based path planning with a meandering path pattern.
Three different setups for the number of sections are considered: 1, 2, or multiple sections.
The same total number of nozzles is controlled across all three section control cases (1, 2, or multiple sections).
Nozzles are controlled either individually or uniformly across the boom bar in the compared setups.
For the 1-section setup, all nozzles along the boom bar uniformly receive the same switch-on or switch-off signal.
For the multiple sections-setup, different sections can receive different switch-on or switch-off signals.
Six different experimental setups are compared in total.
Evaluation metrics include pathlength.
Evaluation metrics include spray volume consumption, which serves as a proxy for overlap area and accounts for nozzle flow rate variations for turn compensation.
Evaluation metrics include overlap area visualization for qualitative verification.

