# ASP-Completeness Proofs of Puzzles Using the T-Metacell Framework


Nattapol Kiatchaipipat
nkiatchaipip@wisc.eduUniversity of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI, USA
Suthee Ruangwises
suthee@cp.eng.chula

**********Extracted claims**********

Pencil puzzles are puzzles that can be solved by writing down solutions on paper, using only logical reasoning.
This paper utilizes the "T-metacell" framework to prove the ASP-completeness of four new pencil puzzles: Grand Tour, Entry Exit, Yagit, and Zahlenschlange.
The results presented in this paper demonstrate how versatile the T-metacell framework is.
The results presented in this paper offer new insights into the computational complexity of problems with various constraints.
Examples of pencil puzzles include Sudoku, Makaro, and Slitherlink.
The computational complexity of pencil puzzles has attracted considerable interest due to their rich connection to constraint satisfaction and graph theory.
Many popular pencil puzzles have been proved to be NP-complete using various techniques.
Puzzles that have been proved to be NP-complete include Five Cells, Goishi Hiroi, Hashiwokakero, Herugolf, Heyawake, Kakuro, Makaro, Numberlink, Slitherlink, Sudoku, Sumplete, Tatamibari, Tilepaint, and Zeiger.
An NP search problem is ASP-complete if for each NP search problem, a polynomial time reduction to it exists along with a polynomial time bijection between the solutions of both problems.
The decision version of an ASP-complete problem is NP-complete.
The counting version of an ASP-complete problem is #P-complete.
The k-ASP P problem, which involves finding another solution given an instance of problem P and k solutions, is NP-complete for any k≥0.
In 2022, Tang proposed a "T-metacell" framework.
The T-metacell framework uses grid graph Hamiltonicity to prove NP-hardness of puzzles.
A T-metacell is any gadget that represents a vertex of degree 3 in a grid graph.
Each T-metacell gadget must have exactly three exits out of its four sides.
Each T-metacell gadget must be reflectable and rotatable.
A path can be made between two T-metacells only when they have their exits aligned.
Solving Hamiltonian cycle on a grid of T-metacells is known to be NP-complete.
Tang’s framework was recently used by Deurloo et al. to prove the NP-completeness of five puzzles.
Tang’s framework was recently used by the MIT Hardness Group to prove the NP-completeness of 37 puzzles.
The MIT Hardness Group proved that solving T-metacell problems is ASP-complete.
The MIT Hardness Group extended the T-metacell framework to allow for T-metacells with one forced exit.
This extension requires the cell to be "asymmetric".
In asymmetric T-metacells, the two unforced exits must be adjacent to each other.
Finding Hamiltonian cycles on a rectangular grid of undirected T-metacells is ASP-complete.
Finding Hamiltonian cycles on a rectangular grid of asymmetric required-edge undirected T-metacells is ASP-complete.
All applicable puzzles follow the common rule of only traversing through orthogonally adjacent cells.
This paper creates a series of reductions that establish the ASP-completeness of Grand Tour, Entry Exit, Yagit, and Zahlenschlange, along with some variants.

