# Pushing the Limits of Frequency Analysis   in Leakage Abuse Attacks


Nathaniel Moyer
)George Mason UniversityUSA, Charalampos Papamanthou
)Yale UniversityUSA and Evgenios M. Kornaropoulos
)George Ma

**********Extracted claims**********

Searchable encryption (SE) is the most scalable cryptographic primitive for searching on encrypted data.
Typical Searchable Encryption (SE) constructions often allow access-pattern leakage.
Access-pattern leakage reveals which encrypted records are retrieved in the server’s responses.
All known generic cryptanalyses assume either that queries are issued uniformly at random or that the attacker observes search-pattern leakage.
Frequency analysis is a cryptanalytic technique used in the context of leakage-abuse attacks on schemes that support encrypted range queries.
Frequency analysis matches the frequency of retrieval of an encrypted record with a plaintext value.
The matching in frequency analysis is based on the plaintext value's probability of retrieval, which follows from knowledge of the query distribution.
LAMa (Leakage-Abuse via Matching) is a generic attack framework.
LAMa works on high-dimensional encrypted data.
A parameterization of LAMa maximizes the reconstructive power of frequency analysis.
This parameterization ensures that an attacker can perform no additional frequency matching to refine the result.
Specific query distributions can make frequency analysis challenging for an attacker.
Such challenging query distributions can act as a mitigation mechanism against frequency analysis.
LAMa has been implemented and benchmarked.
LAMa has reconstructed plaintext data from encrypted range queries spanning up to four dimensions for the first time.
Searchable Encryption (SE) enables searching encrypted data efficiently by revealing a leakage profile.
A leakage profile contains information about the pattern of querying or accessing.
Curtmola et al. introduced the first Searchable Encryption (SE) scheme.
There has been a recent surge in leakage-abuse attacks.
Leakage-abuse attacks aim to reconstruct plaintext databases or queries.
Leakage-abuse attacks use typical leakage profiles.
Prior attacks exploiting access-pattern leakage relied on additional leakage (e.g., search-pattern) or the assumption that client queries come from a specific known distribution (e.g., uniform).
Frequency analysis relies only on knowledge of the query distribution and access pattern.
Kellaris, Kollios, Nissim, and O’Neal proposed the first leakage-abuse attack in the context of range queries.
The adversarial strategy in the Kellaris et al. attack relied heavily on frequency matching.
Frequency matching involves an attacker matching the observed frequency of accessing encrypted records to the theoretical probability of accessing a plaintext value.
The observed frequency is derived from access-pattern leakage.
The theoretical probability is derived from knowledge of the query distribution.
The Kellaris et al. attack assumes that each query issued has the same probability of appearance, which is known as the uniformity assumption.
The Kellaris et al. attack assumes observation of the access-pattern leakage.
An adversary with knowledge of the query distribution can compute the probability of accessing a plaintext value.
Under the uniformity assumption, each encrypted record matches exactly two plaintext candidates: its true value and its reflection.
The concave and symmetric shape of the retrieval probability plot, which results from the uniformity assumption, ensures that only two plaintext values share the same retrieval probability.
An attacker can identify two candidate values for each encrypted record, leading to O(2^n) plausible reconstructions.
The Kellaris et al. attack identifies the record with the lowest frequency.
Under a uniform query distribution, the record with the lowest frequency indicates an extreme value (either the minimum or maximum).
Frequency analysis of simultaneous retrievals helps discard candidates.
Frequency analysis of simultaneous retrievals can narrow down plausible database reconstructions from O(2^n) to 2.
If the guess for the extreme value's extremity is correct, the reconstruction is accurate.
If the guess for the extreme value's extremity is incorrect, the true database is its reflection.
All cryptanalytic techniques that use frequency analysis in one-dimensional plaintext data assume queries are issued uniformly at random.
The uniformity assumption shapes the retrieval probability of plaintexts favorably for the attacker.
Without the uniformity assumption, attacks can yield arbitrarily bad reconstructions.
If the query distribution results in a plaintext access probability as shown in Figure 1-(B), each encrypted record could have as many candidate reconstructions as there are plaintext values.
Calculating the probability that two distinct records are accessed together can yield unique candidate reconstructions.
All prior cryptanalytic approaches that do not assume a uniform query distribution must observe search-pattern leakage.
Search-pattern leakage is an attacker’s ability to determine if encrypted queries are being repeated.
The work by Kornaropoulos et al. is the first attack for one-dimensional ranges that overcomes the uniformity assumption by using the search pattern.
The works by Markatou et al. (DBLP:conf/ccs/MarkatouFTS21) and Falzon et al. (fmacrst-fdrtd-20) for two-dimensional ranges crucially rely on search-pattern leakage.
These works use search-pattern leakage to infer how many distinct ranges return the same response.
The aforementioned works overcome the uniformity assumption by relying on additional leakage.
Recent efforts show that additional leakage can be suppressed.
Only two previous attacks (DBLP:journals/popets/MarkatouFET23 and DBLP:conf/ccs/MarkatouT24) operate in more than two dimensions.
The attack proposed in DBLP:journals/popets/MarkatouFET23 relies on constructions with a non-standard leakage profile.
DBLP:journals/popets/MarkatouFET23 is tailored to constructions that map each plaintext query to multiple encrypted queries.
Such constructions expose the interrelation among tokens.
The attack in DBLP:journals/popets/MarkatouFET23 also relies on the assumption of search-pattern leakage.
The attack presented in DBLP:conf/ccs/MarkatouT24 achieves approximate reconstruction in dimensions beyond two.
The attack presented in DBLP:conf/ccs/MarkatouT24 assumes only access-pattern leakage.
The proposed LAMa attack assumes the adversary knows the client's query distribution.
The proposed LAMa attack targets exact reconstruction.
The proposed LAMa attack outputs reconstructions whose leakage is indistinguishable from that of the true database.
The current work is the first to focus on frequency analysis and push this cryptanalytic technique to its limits.
The current work assumes only access-pattern leakage and knowledge of the query distribution.
The assumed query distribution can differ from uniform.
The current work formalizes the basic ideas of frequency analysis.
The current work generalizes prior frequency analysis attempts.
The unifying framework presented allows any previous and future frequency analysis attack to be cast.
The unifying framework allows direct comparison between frequency analysis approaches.
LAMa abstracts and streamlines the operations to execute frequency analysis.
Connections are drawn for the first time between frequency analysis and Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) solvers.
SMT solvers are used to efficiently identify database reconstructions that satisfy the observed frequencies of retrieval.
LAMa supports frequency analysis for any arbitrary conditional retrieval.
A LAMa prototype has been implemented and evaluated on real-world healthcare datasets.
The LAMa parameterization is proven to allow LAMa to output only databases with the same response distribution as the true database.
Outputting databases with the same response distribution is the optimal outcome achievable through frequency analysis.
A defender can partially mitigate frequency analysis by adjusting the query distribution to flatten the probability of retrievals.
It is impossible to adjust the query distribution so that nothing is revealed unless queries with probability 0 are allowed.
Frequency analysis can effectively reconstruct data when the query distribution is known.
Frequency analysis can effectively reconstruct data even in high dimensions.
Frequency analysis can effectively reconstruct data with only access-pattern leakage.
Controlling the query distribution can greatly increase the number of equally plausible reconstructions.
Increasing the number of equally plausible reconstructions increases adversarial uncertainty.

