**********Extracted claims**********

Any n-point set in d-dimensional Euclidean space (for d=O(1)) admits a (1+)-spanner with O~(n⋅)−d+1 edges.
Any n-point set in d-dimensional Euclidean space (for d=O(1)) admits a (1+)-spanner with O~()−d lightness.
Lightness is a normalized notion of spanner weight, calculated by dividing the spanner weight by the weight of a minimum spanning tree.
For any d where 2≤d=O(1), there exists an n-point set in Rd for which any (1+)-spanner has ~(n⋅)−d+1 edges.
For any d where 2≤d=O(1), there exists an n-point set in Rd for which any (1+)-spanner has ~(n⋅)−d lightness.
Upper bounds for Euclidean spanners rely heavily on the spatial property of cone partitioning in Rd.
The spatial property of cone partitioning in Rd does not extend to the wider family of doubling metrics.
Doubling metrics are metric spaces of constant doubling dimension.
In doubling metrics, the net-tree spanner has O~(n⋅)−d edges.
In doubling metrics, the net-tree spanner can be transformed into a spanner with O~()−(d+1) lightness through pruning redundant edges.
The paper resolves in the negative the question of whether Euclidean bounds for edges (O~(n⋅)−d+1) and lightness (O~()−d) could be achieved in doubling metrics.
The paper presents a simple and tight lower bound.
The net-tree spanner is optimal.
The pruned version of the net-tree spanner is optimal.
A complete weighted graph G_P is induced by a set of n points P in Euclidean space Rd (where d≥2).
In graph G_P, the weight of an edge (x,y) is the Euclidean distance between x and y.
A spanning subgraph H of G_P is a t-spanner for P if the distance in H between any two points x,y in P (d_H(x,y)) is at most t times their Euclidean distance (‖xy‖).
Parameter t is called the stretch of the spanner.
Euclidean spanners were introduced in 1986 by Chew.
Chew's work provided an O(1)-spanner with O(n) edges.
The first constructions of Euclidean (1+)-spanners introduced the -graph.
The -graph was introduced in 2 and 3-dimensional Euclidean spaces.
The -graph was later generalized for any Euclidean space Rd.
The -graph is a variant of the Yao graph.
The Yao graph was introduced by Yao in 1982.
In the Yao graph, the space Rd around each point p is partitioned into cones of a specific angle.
For d=O(1), there are O(−d+1) cones in the Yao graph.
In the Yao graph, edges are added between point p and its closest point in each of these cones.
The -graph is defined similarly to the Yao graph.
In the -graph, points are connected to a point whose orthogonal projection to a fixed ray in the cone is closest.
The stretch of the and Yao graphs is at most 1+ when is chosen as c for a small enough constant c.
The number of cones is asymptotically -d+1 for d=O(1).
The number of cones yields an edge bound of O(n⋅)−d+1.
The greedy spanner and gap-greedy spanner also achieve the tradeoff between 1+ stretch and O(n⋅)−d+1 edges.
Spatial cone partitioning of Rd is key to achieving the O(n⋅)−d+1 size bound in these constructions.
